{"id": "sdqa0044", "pool": "sdqa", "tier": "never", "audio_s": 7.17, "n_chunks": 8, "n_concurrent": 8, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.6965, "eot_read_ms": 27, "reference_answer": "Eleanor Roosevelt", "query": "Who led the writing of the Universal Declaration of Human Rights?", "transcript": "Who led the writing of the Universal Declaration of Human Rights?", "mode": "local", "answer": "The Universal Declaration of Human Rights was drafted by a group of representatives from all regions of the world who gathered at the United Nations in Paris in 1948. Led by Eleanor Roosevelt, the drafting committee included experts in law, human rights, and international relations. Their goal was to create a shared global standard for fundamental human rights that would be universally protected. The declaration consists of 30 articles outlining basic rights and freedoms for all people, regardless of nationality, ethnicity, gender, or religion. It has since become a cornerstone document for human rights advocacy and international law, influencing countless national constitutions and legal frameworks around the world.", "answer_ms": 3805}
