{"id": "sdqa0077", "pool": "sdqa", "tier": "never", "audio_s": 5.4, "n_chunks": 6, "n_concurrent": 6, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.1754, "eot_read_ms": 25, "reference_answer": "Speed describes only how fast an object is moving, whereas velocity gives both how fast it is and in which direction the object is moving", "query": "What is the difference between speed and velocity?", "transcript": "What is the difference between speed and velocity?", "mode": "local", "answer": "Speed and velocity are both measures of how fast something is moving, but they are not the same thing. Speed is a scalar quantity, which means it only has magnitude. It tells you how fast an object is moving, regardless of direction. For example, if you're driving at 60 kilometers per hour, your speed is 60 km/h. Velocity, on the other hand, is a vector quantity. This means it has both magnitude and direction. So, velocity tells you not just how fast you're moving, but also in which direction. Using the same example, if you're driving north at 60 kilometers per hour, your velocity is 60 km/h north. In short, speed is just \"how fast,\" while velocity is \"how fast and in what direction.\"", "answer_ms": 4680}
